    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is his current batting average?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "He wants to raise his batting average to .300. How many of the next consecutive at bats need to be base hits to raise his batting average to .300? Write and solve an equation to describe this situation using \\(x\\) for the number of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Unfortunately, Tyler gets no base hits in his next three at bats. Revise your equation and then calculate how many of his next consecutive at bats need to be base hits to raise his batting average to .300. Be prepared to explain how you…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Elena had 24 base hits in 110 at bats. She has done a lot of practice and now thinks that for all of her future at bats in this season, she will have a batting average of 0.350. If she does, then how many more at bats will Elena need so…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider a student on a school softball team who wants to raise her batting average to .200. So far this year, she has 20 base hits out of 120 at bats, making her current batting average .167 since \\(\\frac{20}{120} = 0.167\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "To increase her batting average, she needs to have more base hits. But each base hit means the number of at bats also increases by 1. Since batting average is the number of base hits divided by the number of at bats, we can use the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align*} .200&=\\tfrac{20+x}{120+x} \\\\ .200 \\boldcdot (120+x) &=\\tfrac{20+x}{120+x} \\boldcdot (120+x) \\\\ 0.2(120+x) &= 20 +x \\\\ 24 + 0.2x &= 20 +x \\\\ 4 &= 0.8x \\\\ 5 &= x \\\\ \\end{align*}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L20-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "20.3: Batting Averages",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Even though we started out with a rational expression on the right side of the equation, multiplying each side by \\((120+x)\\) resulted in an equation similar to ones we have solved before. Checking \\(x=5\\) in our original expression,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l20.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
